@@ -113,9 +113,9 @@ One per figure except the 18 kept originals: 204. Written per part by one agent
 | 1 | `fig-1-2` | Pressing the same key A three times lights three different lamps (F, then E, then C), because each press first steps the fast rotor, so the current takes a new path out through the rotors, turns in the reflector and comes back on a different wire. The reflector never sends a letter back to itself, so no letter is ever enciphered as itself. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-3` | A vacuum tube and a transistor do the same job: a small control signal decides whether a larger current flows and the lamp lights. In the tube an always-hot filament boils off electrons that the grid lets through to the plate; in the transistor the signal goes to the base of a small piece of silicon, with nothing hot to burn out. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-4` | A transistor is a switch worked by its base: with 0 V on the base no current flows between collector and emitter and the lamp stays dark. With 0.7 V on the base, current flows and the lamp lights. |
-| 1 | `fig-1-5` | In a field-effect transistor a gate sits on a thin insulating oxide over p-type silicon, between two n-type regions, the source and the drain. At 0 V there is no path; a positive gate voltage acts through the oxide as an electric field and pulls electrons up into a thin channel, so current flows from source to drain although the gate never touches the electrons. |
+| 1 | `fig-1-5` | In a field-effect transistor a gate sits on a thin insulating oxide over p-type silicon, between two n-type regions, the source and the drain. At 0 V there is no path; a positive gate voltage acts through the oxide as an electric field and draws electrons in from the source and drain into a thin channel, so current flows from source to drain although the gate never touches the electrons. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-6` | On a log scale, transistors per chip rise from 2,300 in the Intel 4004 (1971) to 28 billion in the Apple M4 (2024), twelve million times more. The chips follow a straight dashed line that doubles every two years for more than forty years, then fall below it. |
-| 1 | `fig-1-7` | Both machines flip switches according to instructions. ENIAC (1945) used 17,468 vacuum tubes across a thirty-metre hall, weighed 30 tons, drew 150 kW and lost a tube now and then; the Apple M4 (2024) holds 28 billion transistors on a chip under a fingertip, about 1.6 million times as many switches. |
+| 1 | `fig-1-7` | Both machines flip switches according to instructions. ENIAC (1945) used 17,468 vacuum tubes in thirty metres of cabinets lining a room, weighed 30 tons, drew 150 kW and lost a tube now and then; the Apple M4 (2024) holds 28 billion transistors on a chip under a fingertip, about 1.6 million times as many switches. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-8` | In the von Neumann design one memory holds both the program (load A, add B, store C) and its data (A = 5, B = 3, C = 0), and one shared bus carries each instruction, then its data, to the CPU one transfer at a time. The ALU adds 5 + 3 and the result 8 travels back over the same bus into C. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-9` | Every core runs a loop of four stages: fetch the instruction the program counter (PC) points to, decode it, execute it, then write back the result and move the PC on. In the small program the PC steps from mov eax, 5 to add eax, 3, and jmp 0x00 sends it back to the start, so the loop repeats. |
 | 1 | `fig-1-10` | The assembler turns mov eax, 5 into five bytes, B8 05 00 00 00. The first byte B8 (10111 000) means move a value into a register and names the register as EAX (000), and the next four bytes hold the value 5, lowest byte first, so EAX ends up holding 5. |
