     <h3>Why semiconductors</h3>
 
     <p id="ch1-transistor-p7">
-      A switch with no moving parts cannot open a gap in a wire, so the material itself must stop and start conducting when told. A <em>semiconductor</em>, a material whose conductivity sits between a conductor (like copper) and an insulator (like glass), can be made to. In pure silicon the atoms bond by sharing their outer electrons, so almost none move freely. What changes that is <em>doping</em>: salting the silicon with a trace of another element. An added atom with one more outer electron than silicon has no bond for the extra one, which wanders free (n-type silicon); one with one fewer leaves a gap in a bond, a "hole" (p-type). A field-effect transistor puts p-type silicon between two n-type regions, the source and the drain. Where the kinds meet, free electrons drop into the nearby holes and leave a thin barrier with nothing free to move, so no current flows until a voltage on the gate, which rests on a thin insulating oxide over the p-type silicon, draws free electrons out of the source and drain and under it, where they form a bridge (Fig 1.5). Silicon and oxide both come from sand, which is mostly silicon dioxide: the silicon is refined from it, and the oxide is the same compound.
+      A switch with no moving parts cannot open a gap in a wire, so the material itself must stop and start conducting when told. A <em>semiconductor</em>, a material whose conductivity sits between a conductor (like copper) and an insulator (like glass), can be made to. In pure silicon the atoms bond by sharing their outer electrons, so almost none move freely. What changes that is <em>doping</em>: salting the silicon with a trace of another element. An added atom with one more outer electron than silicon has no bond for the extra one, which wanders free (n-type silicon); one with one fewer leaves a gap in a bond, a "hole" (p-type). A field-effect transistor puts p-type silicon between two n-type regions, the source and the drain. Where the kinds meet, free electrons drop into the nearby holes and leave a thin barrier with nothing free to move, so no current flows. Over the p-type silicon, on a thin insulating oxide, sits the third terminal, the gate, which does the base's job: a voltage on it draws free electrons out of the source and drain and in under the gate, where they form a bridge (Fig 1.5). Silicon and oxide both come from sand, which is mostly silicon dioxide: the silicon is refined from it, and the oxide is the same compound.
     </p>
 
