-      <p id="ch1-transistor-p8" class="diagram-caption">A positive voltage on the gate pulls electrons up under the oxide until they form a thin channel between source and drain, and current flows. The gate never touches the electrons: its charge pulls on them through the insulator, as a rubbed balloon lifts hair, and that influence across a gap, an electric field, gives field-effect transistors their name.</p>
+      <p id="ch1-transistor-p8" class="diagram-caption">A positive voltage on the gate draws electrons in from the source and drain until they form a thin channel under the oxide, and current flows. The gate never touches them: its charge pulls through the insulator, as a rubbed balloon lifts hair, and that influence across a gap, an electric field, names the field-effect transistor.</p>
       <details class="fig-how">
         <summary>How it works</summary>
         <ol>
           <li><b>The silicon.</b> The body is p-type silicon, doped with electron-accepting atoms; the source and drain are n-type, rich in free electrons. With the gate off, nothing joins them.</li>
           <li><b>The field.</b> The gate's voltage acts across the oxide as an electric field. No charge crosses the insulator.</li>
-          <li><b>The channel.</b> The field gathers electrons under the oxide until that thin layer inverts, from p-type to n-type, and conducts.</li>
+          <li><b>The channel.</b> The field gathers electrons, drawn in from the source and drain, under the oxide until that thin layer inverts, from p-type to n-type, and conducts.</li>
           <li><b>The name.</b> MOSFET: metal-oxide-semiconductor field-effect transistor. Every modern CPU is a stack of these about 3 nanometres wide.</li>
         </ol>
       </details>
     </div>
 
     <p id="ch1-transistor-p9">
-      In 1958, Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments made the first <span class="key-term">integrated circuit</span>: a transistor and the other parts of a small circuit, formed in one piece of germanium (another semiconductor) instead of made separately and wired by hand. Within months, Robert Noyce at Fairchild Semiconductor independently did the same on silicon, and his more manufacturable process became the basis of the entire industry. From then on, more parts on a chip meant smaller parts, not more wiring.
+      In 1958, Jack Kilby at Texas Instruments made the first <span class="key-term">integrated circuit</span>: a transistor and the other parts of a small circuit, formed in one piece of germanium (another semiconductor), though still joined by hand with fine wires. Within months, Robert Noyce at Fairchild Semiconductor conceived the same on silicon with the wiring laid on the chip itself, and his more manufacturable process became the basis of the entire industry. From then on, more parts on a chip meant smaller parts, not more wiring.
     </p>
 
